### Why does allOf() return Void?

The input futures may have different types: `CF<String>`, `CF<Integer>`, `CF<BigDecimal>`. Java cannot express a heterogeneous tuple, so allOf returns Void and you collect results individually.

```java
CF<Integer> balance = supplyAsync(() -> getBalance("SAV2001"));
CF<Integer> score = supplyAsync(() -> getCreditScore("SAV2001"));
CF<BigDecimal> property = supplyAsync(() -> getPropertyValue("P001"));

CompletableFuture<Void> allDone = CompletableFuture.allOf(balance, score, property);
allDone.join(); // waits for all, returns null

int b = balance.join();        // already done
int s = score.join();          // already done
BigDecimal p = property.join(); // already done
```

### Strategy 1: Fail-all

If any future fails, allOf().join() throws. The entire operation is rejected.

```java
try {
    CompletableFuture.allOf(balance, score, property).join();
    return decide(balance.join(), score.join(), property.join());
} catch (CompletionException e) {
    return "REJECTED: " + e.getCause().getMessage();
}
```

### Strategy 2: Partial results

Swallow the allOf exception, check each future individually, return what succeeded.

```java
CompletableFuture.allOf(balance, score, property)
    .exceptionally(ex -> null) // swallow
    .join();

Map<String, Object> results = new HashMap<>();
if (!balance.isCompletedExceptionally()) results.put("balance", balance.join());
if (!score.isCompletedExceptionally()) results.put("score", score.join());
if (!property.isCompletedExceptionally()) results.put("property", property.join());
```

### Strategy 3: Fallbacks (most resilient)

Chain exceptionally() on each future before allOf. Every future always succeeds.

```java
CF<Integer> safeBalance = balance.exceptionally(ex -> 0);
CF<Integer> safeScore = score.exceptionally(ex -> 500);
CF<BigDecimal> safeProperty = property.exceptionally(ex -> BigDecimal.ZERO);

CompletableFuture.allOf(safeBalance, safeScore, safeProperty).join();
// All always succeed with real or fallback values
```

### When to use each strategy?

| Strategy | Use case | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|
| Fail-all | Loan decision (needs all real data) | Higher error rate |
| Partial | Dashboard (show what's available) | Incomplete data |
| Fallback | Resilient display (cached defaults) | May show stale data |

### How do you collect homogeneous results?

When all futures have the same type, use a stream.

```java
List<CF<String>> futures = accountIds.stream()
    .map(id -> supplyAsync(() -> fetchBalance(id)))
    .toList();

CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])).join();

List<String> results = futures.stream()
    .map(CompletableFuture::join)
    .toList();
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why not return a tuple? Java does not have tuples. allOf would need a heterogeneous container.
- Does allOf wait for all even if one fails? Yes, allOf waits for all to complete (success or failure) before completing.
- Can you use anyOf for fail-fast? Yes, anyOf completes when the first future completes (success or failure).
